O(n) with potentially two passes over the array. Only works for ASCII strings.

Idea being, use the 8'th bit of a char to mark ISFINAL. ISFINAL means the character is in its final resting place, no further shifts required.

So take "A B C D E"  right rotate by 2:

A B C D E -> E A B C D -> D E A B C 

#define NEXT(i, shift, len)   ((i + shift) % len)
#define PREV(i, shift, len)   ((i - shift + len) % len)

for (i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
  if (ISFINAL(a[i]) {
     locnext = NEXT(i, shift, n);
     locprev = PREV(i, shift, n);
     swap(&a[locnext], &a[locprev]);
     FINALIZE(&a[locnext]);
  } else {
    swap(&a[i], &a[NEXT(i, shift, n)]);
    FINALIZE(&a[i]);
  }
}

// Make another pass and reset 8'th bit for all numbers


So what we did is went through each character and if its not in its Final position, swapped it with its final position.

If it is in its final position, then it must have gotten there by swapping out some other character. So we find this other character by doing PREV(final) and swap it with
where it should be which is NEXT(final)

